### Web API Values Controller REST API Tests with Correlation Tracking
### Base URL - Update the port number to match your local development server
@baseUrl = http://localhost:63124
@serviceUrl = {{baseUrl}}/api/values

### 1. Get All Values (with correlation headers)
GET {{serviceUrl}}
X-Correlation-Id: get-all-values-001
X-Consumer-Id: http-test-client
X-User-Id: tester@example.com
Accept: application/json

###

### 2. Get Specific Value by ID (with correlation headers)
GET {{serviceUrl}}/5
X-Correlation-Id: get-one-value-001
X-Consumer-Id: http-test-client
X-User-Id: tester@example.com
Accept: application/json

###

### 3. Get Value by ID 10
GET {{serviceUrl}}/10
X-Correlation-Id: get-value-10-001
X-Consumer-Id: http-test-client
X-User-Id: tester@example.com
Accept: application/json

###

### 4. POST New Value - With Headers
POST {{serviceUrl}}
X-Correlation-Id: post-value-001
X-Consumer-Id: mobile-app
X-User-Id: developer@example.com
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

"Test Value with Headers"

###

### 5. POST New Value - With Body Parameters (No Headers)
POST {{serviceUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "ConsumerId": "web-portal",
  "UserId": "admin@example.com",
  "value": "Test Value with Body IDs"
}

###

### 6. POST New Value - With Nested Headers
POST {{serviceUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "header": {
    "CorrelationId": "nested-post-001",
    "ConsumerId": "nested-consumer-app",
    "UserId": "nested-user@example.com"
  },
  "value": "Test Value with Nested Headers"
}

###

### 7. PUT Update Value by ID - With Headers
PUT {{serviceUrl}}/5
X-Correlation-Id: put-value-001
X-Consumer-Id: mobile-app
X-User-Id: developer@example.com
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

"Updated Value"

###

### 8. PUT Update Value - With Body Parameters
PUT {{serviceUrl}}/10
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "ConsumerId": "web-portal",
  "UserId": "admin@example.com",
  "value": "Updated Value with IDs"
}

###

### 9. DELETE Value by ID - With Headers
DELETE {{serviceUrl}}/5
X-Correlation-Id: delete-value-001
X-Consumer-Id: mobile-app
X-User-Id: developer@example.com
Accept: application/json

###

### 10. DELETE Value - Auto-Generated Correlation ID
DELETE {{serviceUrl}}/10
X-Consumer-Id: test-client
X-User-Id: tester@example.com
Accept: application/json

###

### 11. Test Sensitive Data Redaction in POST
POST {{serviceUrl}}
X-Correlation-Id: security-test-001
X-Consumer-Id: security-test
X-User-Id: test@example.com
X-API-Key: super-secret-key
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "value": "Test Value",
  "password": "this-should-be-redacted",
  "token": "also-redacted",
  "secret": "hidden-data"
}

###

### 12. Test Auto-Generated Correlation ID (No X-Correlation-Id header)
GET {{serviceUrl}}
X-Consumer-Id: auto-correlation-test
X-User-Id: test@example.com
Accept: application/json

###

### 13. POST with Nested Headers Object (Plural)
POST {{serviceUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "headers": {
    "correlationId": "nested-headers-post-001",
    "consumerId": "nested-headers-app",
    "userId": "headers-user@example.com"
  },
  "value": "Test with Headers Object"
}

###

### 14. PUT with Mixed Headers (Some in header object, some at root)
PUT {{serviceUrl}}/15
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "header": {
    "CorrelationId": "mixed-put-001",
    "ConsumerId": "mixed-consumer"
  },
  "UserId": "root-level-user@example.com",
  "value": "Mixed Headers Update"
}

###

### 15. POST with Complete Nested Headers
POST {{serviceUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "headers": {
    "CorrelationId": "complete-post-001",
    "ConsumerId": "complete-consumer-app",
    "UserId": "complete-user@example.com",
    "RequestSource": "Web Portal",
    "RequestVersion": "2.0.0"
  },
  "value": "Complete Headers Test"
}

###

### 16. GET with PascalCase Correlation Headers
GET {{serviceUrl}}/20
X-Correlation-Id: pascalcase-get-001
X-Consumer-Id: pascal-consumer
X-User-Id: pascal-user@example.com
Accept: application/json

###

### Alternative: Using different port numbers (Update as needed)
# @baseUrl = http://localhost:5000
# @baseUrl = http://localhost:44300
# @baseUrl = https://localhost:44300

###

### NOTES:
### 1. Update the @baseUrl variable with your actual development server URL and port
### 2. The service URL is: {baseUrl}/api/values
### 3. ValuesController Methods:
###    - GET /api/values - Returns array ["value1", "value2"]
###    - GET /api/values/{id} - Returns "value" string
###    - POST /api/values - Accepts string value from body
###    - PUT /api/values/{id} - Accepts string value from body
###    - DELETE /api/values/{id} - Deletes value by ID
### 4. Correlation Headers - THREE ways to provide them:
###    a) HTTP Headers: X-Correlation-Id, X-Consumer-Id, X-User-Id
###    b) Root Level Body: ConsumerId, UserId, CorrelationId (as direct properties)
###    c) Nested Header Object: Inside "header" or "headers" object in request body
### 5. Extraction Priority:
###    - HTTP Headers are checked first
###    - If not found, checks root level of request body
###    - If still not found, checks nested "header" or "headers" object
###    - CorrelationId is auto-generated if not provided anywhere
### 6. Supported Content Types:
###    - JSON: application/json
###    - XML: application/xml or text/xml
### 7. Logging Output:
###    - Check Visual Studio Output window (Debug) to see logged requests/responses
###    - Request path will be parsed to show:
###      * ClassName: "values" (controller name)
###      * OperationName: Action method name or ID
### 8. All requests and responses are logged with correlation tracking
### 9. Sensitive data (passwords, tokens, API keys) are automatically redacted
### 10. To run tests:
###     - Click "Send Request" above each request in Visual Studio
###     - Response will appear in a new editor window
### 11. Response will include X-Correlation-Id header for tracking

### EXAMPLES SUMMARY:
### Examples 1-3: GET requests with correlation headers
### Examples 4-6: POST requests with different header patterns
### Examples 7-8: PUT requests with headers and body IDs
### Examples 9-10: DELETE requests with correlation tracking
### Examples 11-16: Testing various correlation ID patterns and security features

### EXPECTED RESPONSES:
### GET /api/values => ["value1", "value2"]
### GET /api/values/5 => "value"
### POST /api/values => No content (void)
### PUT /api/values/5 => No content (void)
### DELETE /api/values/5 => No content (void)

### LOGGING EXAMPLE:
### Request to GET /api/values will log:
### {
###   "Request": {
###     "Path": "/api/values",
###     "ClassName": "values",
###     "OperationName": null,
###     "Method": "GET",
###     ...
###   }
### }
###
### Request to GET /api/values/5 will log:
### {
###   "Request": {
###     "Path": "/api/values/5",
###     "ClassName": "values",
###     "OperationName": "5",
###     "Method": "GET",
###     ...
###   }
### }
